The spec says BCP 47 tags — underscores are not valid BCP 47. I should reject input containing characters outside the BCP 47 grammar before parsing. Strict approach: validate the raw tag matches BCP 47 syntax: alphanumeric subtags separated by hyphens, with length constraints. Simplest robust check: reject if raw contains anything not in [A-Za-z0-9-], and also reject empty subtags (leading/trailing/double hyphens). Then use language.Parse for deeper well-formedness.

Actually a regex like `^[A-Za-z]{2,3}(-...)*` gets complicated. Better: check each hyphen-separated subtag is 1-8 alphanumeric chars — that's the BCP 47 grammar skeleton. Then language.Parse validates semantics. But language.Parse also accepts grandfathered tags like "i-klingon" — subtags "i" (1 char) and "klingon" — passes the skeleton check, and Parse accepts. Good.

